      {
        "heading": "8 DISCUSSION",
        "text": "In this work we have theoretically and empirically studied the ability of simple NN modules to specialize and acquire systematic knowledge. We found that this ability is challenging even in our simple setting. Neither implicit biases in learning dynamics, nor all but the most stringent modularity, caused networks to exploit compositional sub-structure in the data. Our results complement recent empirical studies, helping to highlight the complex factors influencing systematic generalization. Our work also highlights a potential problem with the use of architectural priors to impose modularity: the architecture must be perfectly tailored to the given dataset. This leads to the already established difficulties associated with modular architectures where it is difficult to get modules to specialize in order to become systematic. Thus, we point towards a further consideration: which inductive biases are flexible enough to achieve systematicity without being tailored to a specific problem? A neuroscientific perspective is that sparsity plays a large role in producing systematic representations, particularly in the cerebellum (Cayco-Gajic & Silver, 2019). Thus, exploring the utility of sparsity inducing methods as a means of achieving systematicity without architectural priors is an important direction of future work. Ultimately, we hope that the formal perspective provided in this work will lead to greater understanding of these phenomena and aid the design of improved, modular, learning systems."
